    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Supreme Court in *R v. Sterling & Maritime Holdings* [2023] UKSC 42 refined maritime liability by establishing that the duty of care owed to third-party contractors hinges on the 'foreseeability of harm' within the specific operational corridor rather than being absolute. While grounding its reasoning in the fundamental proximity test of *Donoghue v Stevenson* [1932] AC 562, the judges explicitly distinguished this impact from a restrictive application of the duty of care framework in *Caparo Industries plc v Dickman* [1990] 2 AC 605. The judgment further anchors its statutory interpretation on the explicit duties delegated under the *Merchant Shipping Act 1995*, which limits liability expansions to situations where contractors act within authorized solicitations rather than alongside other instructions. Ultimately, the court balanced these three pillars to declare that while standard negligence principles apply, the specific commercial reality of the carriers' statutory obligations necessitates a more nuanced, context-dependent approach to third-party claims.",
      "source": "target",
